(117, 239, 7, 'Screening call went average overall. Candidate has relevant project management experience and answered most questions clearly, but the delivery felt somewhat scripted at times instead of conversational.\n\nNeed improvement in communication flow and answer delivery to make the discussion feel more natural and engaging with the interviewer.\n\nWith more confident and interactive communication, the overall interview presence can improve significantly.', '2026-05-06 17:17:50'),
(118, 235, 9, 'Done.', '2026-05-06 17:43:46'),
(119, 229, 8, 'screening', '2026-05-06 17:54:20'),
(120, 234, 10, 'interview was overall average. just need confidence while answering questions and also speak in natural language does not recite answers from document', '2026-05-06 18:21:09'),
(121, 238, 10, 'interview was average overall', '2026-05-06 18:21:48'),
(122, 245, 7, 'The interviewer explained that the company builds a large-scale IoT connectivity orchestration platform integrating 300+ global carriers, eSIM providers, satellite networks, and edge routers into a single unified system.\n\nHe also mentioned they are currently doing extensive hardware and connectivity testing for eSIM-enabled IoT devices and felt your embedded firmware and hardware debugging background could align well with that work.', '2026-05-06 18:26:50'),
(123, 231, 15, 'interview went well.the candidate communicated ideas clearly and confidently, showed strong subject knowledge, and handled questions thoughtfully.', '2026-05-06 18:29:02'),
(124, 244, 9, 'Done.', '2026-05-06 18:33:18'),
(125, 240, 11, 'The candidate came across as strong and practical, with solid hands-on experience in SAP, forecasting, and variance analysis. He explained concepts clearly, connected well with the interviewer’s workflow, and showed a good balance of technical understanding and business context.\n\nNext steps would likely be a second round focusing deeper on SAP specifics, S&OP cycles, and stakeholder scenarios, or a panel interview with team members to assess real-world problem solving and fit within cross-functional teams.', '2026-05-06 18:58:19'),
(126, 242, 7, '***', '2026-05-06 18:59:27'),
(127, 242, 7, 'Screening call went smoothly and mainly covered your Azure experience, client-facing work, relocation, security exposure, and salary expectations.\ncandidate answered confidently and kept the conversation natural and professional throughout the call.\ncandidate background in Azure infrastructure, automation, and cloud operations aligned well with the role requirements and likely left a positive impression.', '2026-05-06 19:00:20'),
(128, 225, 7, 'i am done with this task updating', '2026-05-06 19:43:48'),
(129, 225, 7, 'N/A', '2026-05-06 19:44:24'),
(130, 236, 9, 'Done.', '2026-05-06 19:55:40'),
(131, 237, 11, 'Interview went well. He showed hands-on experience with production ML systems, especially around model lifecycle, PySpark pipelines, and RAG-based LLM solutions. Communication was clear and practical, showing good understanding of real-world trade-offs, though deeper detail on CatBoost-specific optimization and existing model debugging could strengthen the profile.\n \nNext Steps: Move forward to the final round with a 1-hour technical interview including a live coding exercise (Python/PySpark) and discussion on maintaining and improving existing production ML models.', '2026-05-06 19:57:59'),
(132, 241, 11, 'The candidate showed strong hands-on experience in SOC Operations, Incident Response, Splunk investigations, and Threat Hunting, and answered questions confidently with clear real-world examples from his professional experience. He communicated naturally throughout the interview and did not rely on the Google document provided by the support person, instead answering questions independently based on his own knowledge and experience.', '2026-05-06 19:58:32'),
(133, 246, 7, 'The interview went really well overall. The discussion was very conversational and focused mainly on understanding her FP&A experience, forecasting thought process, and how I approach business problems from a financial perspective. They seemed to like her answers around forecasting, product analysis, and franchise profitability reasoning.\n\ninterviewer also mentioned that he got a great impression from the conversation and said he would be sharing positive feedback with the team. He mentioned that I would most likely be contacted soon regarding the next round of the interview process.', '2026-05-06 19:58:34'),
(134, 227, 11, 'Interview Completed. yesterday', '2026-05-06 19:59:02'),
(135, 226, 11, 'Interview Got Cancelled', '2026-05-06 19:59:31'),
(136, 222, 8, 'Resecdule', '2026-05-06 20:04:20'),
(137, 232, 11, 'Interview went well. He showed hands-on experience in building AI/ML systems, especially around RAG pipelines, APIs, and real-time data processing, and was able to explain concepts clearly with practical examples. Communication was confident and natural, though some answers could be slightly more concise and business-impact focused.\n \nNext steps: Move the candidate to the next round for a deeper technical evaluation, possibly including system design or hands-on coding focused on API + GenAI use cases.', '2026-05-07 13:58:30'),
(138, 250, 10, 'no one joined', '2026-05-07 14:40:26'),
(139, 228, 10, 'interview was good overall', '2026-05-07 14:40:57'),
(140, 249, 8, 'Technical round', '2026-05-07 14:46:16'),
(141, 247, 7, 'System Design Round:\nInterview went average overall. Candidate had good understanding of distributed systems, streaming pipelines, and Customer 360 architecture concepts, but looked slightly nervous and a little confused while structuring parts of the system design discussion. Communication improved as the discussion progressed, and the candidate was able to answer follow-up questions around DynamoDB, stream processing, and batch pipelines reasonably well.\n\nCoding Round:\nCoding round went well overall. Candidate communicated clearly during problem solving, handled follow-up optimization questions confidently, and was able to improve the solution from heap-based approach to bucket sort with linear time complexity. Interviewer seemed impressed with the thought process, optimization discussion, and ability to explain Java concepts while coding.', '2026-05-07 15:15:01'),
