(90, 377, 0, '2', 'Commonfund', 'Rajesh Sharma', 4, 4, 3, 4, 'Yes', 'The candidate handled the interview quite well, especially considering the interviewer was an Indian interviewer doing deep cross-questioning and continuously challenging the answers from different angles. He stayed calm, explained concepts practically, and showed real production understanding instead of giving textbook AI answers.\n\nHis strongest areas were AI orchestration, backend workflows, MCP integrations, and system-level thinking. A few frontend and theoretical areas needed more confidence and crispness, but overall, he came across as someone who has genuinely worked on real AI systems and can grow well in a strategic AI engineering role.', '', 3.75, '2026-05-19 20:33:35', '2026-05-19 20:33:35'),
(91, 385, 0, '1', 'Discovery Machine', 'Sam', 4, 4, 3, 4, 'Yes', 'Interview went well. He came across as technically strong, collaborative, and genuinely interested in the company’s mission. He communicated well, showed real hands-on QA automation experience, and seemed comfortable working in evolving engineering environments where process improvement and ownership are important.\n\nNext steps would likely be moving him into the technical interview round to evaluate deeper automation knowledge, framework design thinking, debugging approach, and how he handles complex system-level QA challenges.', '', 3.75, '2026-05-20 15:30:50', '2026-05-20 15:30:50'),
(92, 393, 0, '2', 'transcendstreet', 'Kiran', 3, 3, 3, 3, 'Yes', 'The candidate showed strong backend and problem-solving skills with good knowledge of Java, Spring Boot, microservices, and distributed systems. He communicated confidently, explained concepts clearly, and demonstrated practical experience working on scalable financial applications.', '', 3.00, '2026-05-20 22:22:35', '2026-05-20 22:22:35'),
